rential/14042594/assets/fc1f5e662d05224ca25f4893c62389cc5d8b11acbae73f269a7921e097374b85.png) gives\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/683610152cf39c63fcf62ccdeadf1beed4ff8ef1cfb6a28452420974901cdd0c.png)\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/7ce940ebe652683318187b453d18127a4334b5c0dfef68da5e9a74e937f2d734.png)\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index equals ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/0021ff77f0c4565f235da704e0b4d45d1145486e46d721ea834cba941061b020.png) since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/ea7eeb2aa3909170b1ed83f408f8b17ec17d5012db54fd49910e85034899dbb8.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6672"] :question/problem "Let $y =\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}$ be a series solution to the equation $2y''+y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for $a_n$ can be written as\n\n$$\na_{n+2} = \\dfrac{ a_n }{ f(n) }, \\qquad n= 0,1,2\\ldots\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $y'(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find $y''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y''(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty n (n-1) a_{n} x^{n-2} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+2) (m+1) a_{m+2} x^{m} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty (n+2) (n+1) a_{n+2} x^{n} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[2(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}+{a}_{n}{x}^{n}] & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[2(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}+{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n2(n+2)(n+1) a_{n+2}  +a_n = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+2} = -\\dfrac{ a_n }{ 2(n+1)(n+2)  }, \\qquad n= 0,1,2 \\ldots\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(n)=-2(n+1)(n+2).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
